  "statement": "PROJECTION on the BPU side (device-model constants, E_ADC = FoM_W x 2^b with FoM_W 10 / 50 fJ per conversion-step), MEASURED on the digital side. Same 2.7M-parameter nanoGPT. GPU INT8 measured (NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Laptop GPU): 256077 nJ/token incl. idle power, 169377 above idle, val loss gap +0.05%; FP32 179763 nJ/token. H100 INT8 published: 1991.6 nJ/token at peak, 4979.0 at 40% utilisation (not measured). BPU inference nJ/token (val-loss gap vs ideal): tile FoM 10 fJ 6-bit 91 (+1.4%); tile FoM 10 fJ 8-bit 257 (-0.6%); tile FoM 50 fJ 6-bit 313 (+1.4%); tile FoM 50 fJ 8-bit 1146 (-0.6%); layer FoM 10 fJ 6-bit 44 (+1.8%); layer FoM 10 fJ 8-bit 71 (-0.8%); layer FoM 50 fJ 6-bit 80 (+1.8%); layer FoM 50 fJ 8-bit 213 (-0.8%); lif FoM 10 fJ 6-bit 2749 (+1.9%); lif FoM 10 fJ 8-bit 11567 (-0.8%); lif FoM 50 fJ 6-bit 2749 (+1.9%); lif FoM 50 fJ 8-bit 11567 (-0.8%). Cheapest lossless cell (gap <= 2%): layer fom10 6-bit at 44 nJ/token = 0.01x H100@40%, 5797x below the measured laptop GPU; MACs alone 11.3 nJ/token; LIF spike rate 55.3 (8-bit window) / 13.0 (6-bit) spikes per output per token; converter ranges at the 99.9th percentile. Scaling: conversions grow as layers x d_model while MACs grow as parameters (~ layers x d_model^2), so the BPU / H100@40% ratio for the per-layer 8-bit FoM-10 cell falls from 0.01 at 2.7M to 0.002 at 1T: the relative advantage grows with model size because the converter cost per MAC shrinks as 1/d_model."
